1
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:11,266
Let us start with the graphs that have as many edges as they possibly can.

2
00:00:11,266 --> 00:00:18,490
In a complete graph every pair of distinct vertices is joined by an edge, so nothing can be added.

3
00:00:18,490 --> 00:00:23,986
So here are the complete graphs on two, three and four vertices.

4
00:00:24,350 --> 00:00:31,762
Counting the edges gives one, three and six, and the complete graph on five vertices has ten.

5
00:00:31,762 --> 00:00:37,098
It is the only complete graph on five vertices, so we call it K five.

6
00:00:37,098 --> 00:00:42,432
Each of its vertices touches the four others, so its degree is four.

7
00:00:42,432 --> 00:00:46,812
With n vertices that becomes n minus one.

8
00:00:47,183 --> 00:00:51,222
Adding up all the degrees gives n times n minus one,

9
00:00:51,222 --> 00:00:56,039
and this counts every edge twice because an edge has two ends.

10
00:00:56,039 --> 00:01:01,081
So the number of edges is n times n minus one over two.

11
00:01:01,081 --> 00:01:06,369
For K five that is five times four over two, which is ten.

12
00:01:08,050 --> 00:01:14,608
A tree is a connected graph that contains no cycles, and here is one with six vertices.

13
00:01:14,608 --> 00:01:20,936
Next to it stands a graph that is connected too, but four of its vertices form a cycle.

14
00:01:20,936 --> 00:01:25,456
So the first graph is a tree and the second one is not.

15
00:01:25,816 --> 00:01:27,708
Now count the tree.

16
00:01:27,708 --> 00:01:33,948
It has six vertices and five edges, always one edge fewer than vertices.

17
00:01:33,948 --> 00:01:41,352
So a tree is also a connected graph with n minus one edges, and that is the second way to say it.

18
00:01:41,716 --> 00:01:47,414
Now cut any single edge of the tree, and it falls into two separate pieces.

19
00:01:47,414 --> 00:01:51,322
So nothing can be removed without losing the connection.

20
00:01:51,322 --> 00:01:59,776
In the same way, adding one new edge between vertices four and five closes a triangle, which is a cycle.

21
00:02:00,150 --> 00:02:05,720
A complete graph is unique for each number of vertices, but trees are not.

22
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:12,628
A formula counts them, and there are n to the power n minus two trees on n vertices.

23
00:02:12,628 --> 00:02:16,273
For six vertices that is six to the fourth power,

24
00:02:16,273 --> 00:02:20,812
which is one thousand two hundred ninety six different trees.

25
00:02:22,483 --> 00:02:28,197
A rooted tree has one special vertex called the root, which we draw at the top.

26
00:02:28,197 --> 00:02:33,957
Below it the tree spreads out, and the vertices at the very bottom are the leaves.

27
00:02:33,957 --> 00:02:38,065
The ones in between are the internal nodes.

28
00:02:38,416 --> 00:02:46,854
In a rooted tree the root is never a leaf, whereas in an unrooted tree a leaf is any vertex of degree one.

29
00:02:46,854 --> 00:02:53,550
The depth is the length of the longest path from the root to a leaf, which here is three edges.

30
00:02:53,550 --> 00:02:59,286
Level k holds the vertices that are exactly k edges from the root.

31
00:02:59,650 --> 00:03:07,282
The levels hold one, two, four and eight vertices, because every vertex has two below it.

32
00:03:07,282 --> 00:03:13,570
That is exactly how a bacterium divides into two new bacteria, layer after layer.

33
00:03:13,570 --> 00:03:18,124
Binary search trees use the same shape for fast searching,

34
00:03:18,124 --> 00:03:22,522
and hard problems like Maximum Cut become easy on trees.

35
00:03:24,183 --> 00:03:29,626
Now we can prove that the first two definitions of a tree really are equivalent,

36
00:03:29,626 --> 00:03:31,735
which is Theorem ten point two.

37
00:03:31,735 --> 00:03:39,017
It says that a graph is connected with no cycles exactly when it is connected with n minus one edges.

38
00:03:39,017 --> 00:03:44,269
So there are two directions to check, and we take the forward one first.

39
00:03:44,650 --> 00:03:50,194
For the forward direction we use strong induction on the number of vertices n.

40
00:03:50,194 --> 00:03:58,292
The base case is a single vertex, which has no edges at all, and zero is exactly one minus one.

41
00:03:58,292 --> 00:04:02,314
So the claim holds for n equal to one.

42
00:04:02,683 --> 00:04:07,713
For the inductive step, suppose the claim holds up to k vertices.

43
00:04:07,713 --> 00:04:16,675
Now take a connected graph G with no cycles on k plus one vertices, and remove one vertex v with its edges.

44
00:04:16,675 --> 00:04:23,679
Call what is left G prime, and notice that removing a vertex can never create a cycle.

45
00:04:24,050 --> 00:04:29,384
Assume that G prime is still connected, which is the case the notes treat,

46
00:04:29,384 --> 00:04:32,412
and the other case is left as an exercise.

47
00:04:32,412 --> 00:04:37,111
Then G prime is connected with no cycles on k vertices,

48
00:04:37,111 --> 00:04:41,810
so the induction hypothesis gives it k minus one edges.

49
00:04:42,183 --> 00:04:44,125
Now put v back.

50
00:04:44,125 --> 00:04:51,001
If v had two edges or more, then the connected graph G prime would give a cycle through v.

51
00:04:51,001 --> 00:04:55,667
But G has no cycles, so v has exactly one edge.

52
00:04:55,667 --> 00:05:02,891
That makes k minus one plus one, which is k edges, exactly as the claim needs.

53
00:05:04,550 --> 00:05:07,972
For the converse we argue by contradiction.

54
00:05:07,972 --> 00:05:15,086
Suppose a graph G is connected, has n minus one edges, and still contains a cycle.

55
00:05:15,086 --> 00:05:21,744
Then removing one edge of that cycle leaves G connected, with n minus two edges.

56
00:05:22,116 --> 00:05:29,628
But a connected graph needs at least n minus one edges, which is a fact the notes leave as an exercise.

57
00:05:29,628 --> 00:05:36,578
So n minus two edges are too few, we have a contradiction, and the converse is proved.

